@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+# Privacy by Design — The Daily Grind website
+
+## Principles applied
+| Principle | Implementation |
+|---|---|
+| Privacy by default | Optional processing stays off until the user opts in (`assets/js/privacy.js`). No consent banner because nothing optional runs by default. |
+| Respect browser signals | Global Privacy Control and Do Not Track force all optional categories off and lock the toggles. Declared in `/.well-known/gpc.json`. |
+| Data minimization | The client stores one `localStorage` record, `dg_privacy`: `{v, d, c}` (schema version, day-precision date, booleans). No IDs, cookies, or fingerprinting. It expires after 180 days. |
+| No third parties | All assets are first-party. The hero photo (Unsplash, Unsplash License) is hosted locally and has no EXIF metadata. Visitor IPs and referrers no longer go to Unsplash. |
+| Secure data handling | Logout is a POST form. It clears `sessionStorage`, `dg_*` keys, and Cache Storage on the client. The server sends `Clear-Site-Data`. Signed-in routes are `Cache-Control: no-store`. |
+| Least privilege | Strict CSP (`default-src 'none'`, no inline code). `Permissions-Policy` turns off camera, microphone, geolocation, Topics, and more. COOP/CORP/COEP isolation. |
+
+## Security headers
+`_headers` is the source of truth (Netlify / Cloudflare Pages format; copy it into your proxy config on other hosts). `index.html` has a `<meta>` CSP and a referrer policy as a fallback for hosts that can't send headers, such as the local `statikk` server. `frame-ancestors`, HSTS and `Clear-Site-Data` only work as real headers.
+
+## Third-party register
+| Origin | Purpose | Data shared | DPA | Status |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| _(none)_ | — | — | — | — |
+
+To add a vendor:
+1. Do a privacy review and sign a DPA.
+2. Add the vendor to this table and to the privacy policy.
+3. Add its origin to `THIRD_PARTY_ALLOWLIST` in `assets/js/privacy.js` and `ALLOWED_THIRD_PARTIES` in `scripts/privacy-audit.mjs`.
+4. Add it to both CSPs.
+5. If it is non-essential, load it only after `DailyGrindPrivacy.hasConsent('<category>')`.
+
+## Auditing
+- **Static:** `npm run audit:privacy` (also runs before `npm start`). It fails on unapproved origins, tracker or fingerprinting signatures, inline code that would weaken the CSP, GET forms carrying personal data, GET logout, tabnabbing, CSP drift between the meta tag and `_headers`, and EXIF/IPTC metadata in images.
+- **Runtime:** `privacy.js` logs a console warning for any request to an origin that isn't allowlisted, and for any CSP violation. `DailyGrindPrivacy.auditReport()` returns a summary.
